.TH rsh "" "Command"
.SH NAME
rsh \- restricted command interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrsh [\-ceiknprstuvx] \fIfile \fR[\fIargument ...\fR]
.br
\fBrsh [\-eiknprstuvx] \-c \fIstring \fR[\fIargument ...\fR]
.br
\fBrsh [\-eiknprstuvx]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
rsh -- restricted command interpreter
Usage:	rsh [ -ceiknprstuvx ] [ -c string ] [ file [ argument ... ] ]
Options:
	-c string	Execute 'string' as a command
	-e	Exit if any command exits with nonzero status
	-i	Be an interactive shell
	-k	Place all keyword (name=value) arguments into the environment
	-n	Read commands but do not execute them
	-p	Reserved (meaning not documented in the source)
	-r	Mark this session as a restricted shell
	-s	Read commands from the standard input, write output to the
		standard error
	-t	Exit after reading and executing one command
	-u	Treat use of an unset variable as an error
	-v	Print each input line as it is read
	-x	Print each command and its arguments as it is executed
	-	(alone) Cancel the -v and -x options
.HE
.B rsh
is built from the same command-interpreter sources as
.BR sh ,
compiled as a separate program from a copy of those sources kept
under its own name; it accepts the same option letters and the same
command language.
With no
.I file
and without
.B \-c
or
.BR \-s ,
.B rsh
reads commands from its standard input.
With a
.I file
argument (and neither
.B \-c
nor
.BR \-s ),
.B rsh
reads commands from
.IR file ,
and any further arguments become that script's positional
parameters.
With
.BR \-c ,
.B rsh
executes
.I string
as a command, and any further arguments become its positional
parameters.
.PP
Option letters may be combined after a single leading
.BR \- ,
as in
.BR \-ex .
.IP "\fB\-c \fIstring\fR"
Execute
.I string
as a command.
.IP \fB\-e\fR
Exit as soon as any command exits with nonzero status, if not
running interactively.
.IP \fB\-i\fR
Be interactive (prompt, and ignore some signals) even if the
standard input is not a terminal.
.IP \fB\-k\fR
Place keyword (\fIname\fB=\fIvalue\fR) arguments anywhere in a
command line into the environment of that command, not only those
that precede the command name.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Read commands and check their syntax, but do not execute them.
.IP \fB\-p\fR
Recorded in the shell's flag array by letter, but its purpose is not
documented in this source (the header comment marks it ``Unknown'').
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Mark the session as a restricted shell.
See NOTES.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Read commands from the standard input even if a
.I file
or
.B \-c
.I string
was also given, and write ordinary command output to the standard
error instead of the standard output.
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Read and execute a single command, then exit.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Treat reference to an unset shell variable as an error, instead of
substituting a null value.
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Print each line of input as it is read, before any substitution.
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Print each command and its expanded arguments just before executing
it.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR login ,
.B sh
.SH NOTES
The
.B \-r
flag is recorded in the shell's flag array (as the character
.B r
under
.BR rflag ),
exactly like every other option letter, but no other routine in this
source examines
.B rflag
again afterward: nothing in this source consults it to refuse a
.BR cd ,
a command named with a
.BR / ,
output redirection, or reassignment of any variable.
Giving
.B \-r
to this build has no effect beyond being recorded.
